window.docContents[24].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___11___-Tensor-products/Properties-of-tensor-products___-universal-property___-basis___-duals___-symmetric-and-exterior-powers/#Chapter2___Problem2___11___3___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.11. Tensor products\u0009Properties of tensor products: universal property, basis, duals, symmetric and exterior powers\u0009Properties of tensor products: universal property, basis, duals, symmetric and exterior powers","header":"Formalization","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___11___-Tensor-products/Properties-of-tensor-products___-universal-property___-basis___-duals___-symmetric-and-exterior-powers/#Chapter2___Problem2___11___3___formalization"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___10___-Representations-of-tensor-products/Tensor-product-of-matrix-algebras/#Chapter3___Exercise3___10___1":{"contents":"Exercise 3.10.1. Show that \\operatorname{Mat}_m(k) \\otimes \\operatorname{Mat}_n(k) \\cong \\operatorname{Mat}_{mn}(k).\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.10. Representations of tensor products\u0009Tensor product of matrix algebras","header":"Tensor product of matrix algebras","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___10___-Representations-of-tensor-products/Tensor-product-of-matrix-algebras/#Chapter3___Exercise3___10___1"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___1___-Subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations/Completion-of-alternative-proof-of-Proposition-3___1___4/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___1___-Subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations--Completion-of-alternative-proof-of-Proposition-3___1___4--Completion-of-alternative-proof-of-Proposition-3___1___4--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"For an internal family of simple submodules, a surjective linear map agrees on the associated submodule with a suitable map.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Discussion_after_Lemma3.1.6; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Lemma3.1.6; role=supporting]\n\nA submodule of the displayed direct sum admits bounded multiplicities and linearly independent\ncoordinate data satisfying the stated expansion formula.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Discussion_after_Lemma3.1.6/Derived4; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Proposition3.1.4; role=supporting]\n\nA submodule of the displayed direct sum admits bounded multiplicities and injective coordinate data.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Discussion_after_Lemma3.1.6/Derived4; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Proposition3.1.4; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Remark3.1.5; role=primary]\n\nAt an index and a map from the indexed module, the induced multiplicity map is composition with the original module homomorphism.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Discussion_after_Lemma3.1.6/Derived2; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Discussion_alternative_proof_of_Proposition3.1.4; role=supporting]\n\nThe restriction of scalars of a module map is reconstructed by decomposing the source, applying the induced multiplicity maps componentwise, and recombining the target.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Discussion_after_Lemma3.1.6/Derived2; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.1. Subrepresentations in semisimple representations\u0009Completion of alternative proof of Proposition 3.1.4\u0009Completion of alternative proof of Proposition 3.1.4\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___1___-Subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations/Completion-of-alternative-proof-of-Proposition-3___1___4/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___1___-Subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations--Completion-of-alternative-proof-of-Proposition-3___1___4--Completion-of-alternative-proof-of-Proposition-3___1___4--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___3___-Representations-of-direct-sums-of-matrix-algebras/Irreducible-representations-of-direct-sums-of-matrix-algebras/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___3___-Representations-of-direct-sums-of-matrix-algebras--Irreducible-representations-of-direct-sums-of-matrix-algebras--Irreducible-representations-of-direct-sums-of-matrix-algebras--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"Equivalent coordinate-vector spaces carrying the first auxiliary module structure have equal indices when all indicated sizes are nonzero.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Theorem3.3.1; role=primary]\n\nEach selected coordinate-vector module over the displayed algebra is simple when all indicated sizes are nonzero.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Discussion_proof_of_Theorem3.3.1; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Theorem3.3.1; role=primary]\n\nEvery finite-dimensional module over the displayed algebra is equivalent to a direct sum of finite families of coordinate-vector modules.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Discussion_proof_of_Theorem3.3.1; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.3.3/Derived17; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Remark3.3.4/Derived8; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Theorem3.3.1; role=primary]\n\nEvery finite-dimensional simple module over the displayed algebra is equivalent to one of its coordinate-vector modules.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Theorem3.3.1; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.3. Representations of direct sums of matrix algebras\u0009Irreducible representations of direct sums of matrix algebras\u0009Irreducible representations of direct sums of matrix algebras\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___3___-Representations-of-direct-sums-of-matrix-algebras/Irreducible-representations-of-direct-sums-of-matrix-algebras/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___3___-Representations-of-direct-sums-of-matrix-algebras--Irreducible-representations-of-direct-sums-of-matrix-algebras--Irreducible-representations-of-direct-sums-of-matrix-algebras--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___13___-Proof-of-the-classification-theorem-for-representations-of-___S_n___/End-of-proof-of-Lemma-5___13___1-_LPAR_statement-on-missing-page_RPAR_/#Chapter5___Discussion_end_of_Lemma5___13___1_proof":{"contents":"Any two elements in the first row of T must be in different columns of T', so there exists q'_1 \\in Q'_\\lambda which moves all these elements to the first row. So there is p_1 \\in P_\\lambda such that p_1 T and q'_1 T' have the same first row. Now do the same procedure with the second row, finding elements p_2, q'_2 such that p_2 p_1 T and q'_2 q'_1 T' have the same first two rows. Continuing so, we will construct the desired elements p, q'. The lemma is proved. \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.13. Proof of the classification theorem for representations of $S_n$\u0009End of proof of Lemma 5.13.1 (statement on missing page)","header":"End of proof of Lemma 5.13.1 (statement on missing page)","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___13___-Proof-of-the-classification-theorem-for-representations-of-___S_n___/End-of-proof-of-Lemma-5___13___1-_LPAR_statement-on-missing-page_RPAR_/#Chapter5___Discussion_end_of_Lemma5___13___1_proof"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___23___-Algebraic-representations-of-___GL_LPAR_V_RPAR____/#chapter-05___section-5-23":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results","header":"5.23. Algebraic representations of $GL(V)$","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___23___-Algebraic-representations-of-___GL_LPAR_V_RPAR____/#chapter-05___section-5-23"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___8___-Complexes-and-cohomology/Short-exact-sequence/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___8___-Complexes-and-cohomology--Short-exact-sequence--Short-exact-sequence--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"Converts auxiliary categorical data to zero-morphism data indexed by the endpoints of a short complex.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter7/Definition7.8.2; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.8. Complexes and cohomology\u0009Short exact sequence\u0009Short exact sequence\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___8___-Complexes-and-cohomology/Short-exact-sequence/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___8___-Complexes-and-cohomology--Short-exact-sequence--Short-exact-sequence--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___2___-Projective-covers/Hom-from-projective-cover-computes-Jordan-Holder-multiplicity/#Chapter9___Proposition9___2___3":{"contents":"Proposition 9.2.3. Let N be any finite dimensional A-module. Then one has \\dim \\operatorname{Hom}_A(P_i, N) = [N : M_i], the multiplicity of occurrence on M_i in the Jordan-Hölder series of N.\n\nProof. If N = M_j, the statement is clear. Also, if\n\n0 \\to N_1 \\to N_2 \\to N_3 \\to 0\n\nis an exact sequence of A-modules, then the corresponding sequence\n\n0 \\to \\operatorname{Hom}_A(P_i, N_1) \\to \\operatorname{Hom}_A(P_i, N_2) \\to \\operatorname{Hom}_A(P_i, N_3) \\to 0\n\nis exact, as P_i is projective. This implies the statement. \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.2. Projective covers\u0009Hom from projective cover computes Jordan-Holder multiplicity","header":"Hom from projective cover computes Jordan-Holder multiplicity","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___2___-Projective-covers/Hom-from-projective-cover-computes-Jordan-Holder-multiplicity/#Chapter9___Proposition9___2___3"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___5___-Blocks/Blocks-and-indecomposable-central-idempotents/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___5___-Blocks--Blocks-and-indecomposable-central-idempotents--Blocks-and-indecomposable-central-idempotents--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"An indecomposable finite-length module admits a simple module satisfying the displayed condition.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Problem9.5.3; role=supporting]\n\nMorphisms between modules are subsingleton under the displayed simple-module conditions.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Problem9.5.3; role=supporting]\n\nThe associated type for the monoid algebra has cardinality two.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Problem9.5.3; role=supporting]\n\nIn characteristic two, the monoid algebra is algebra-equivalent to a product of two-by-two matrices and the auxiliary algebra.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Problem9.5.3; role=supporting]\n\nEvery simple module object over the monoid algebra is isomorphic to one of the two distinguished module objects.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Problem9.5.3; role=supporting]\n\nAn equivalence between finite-length parameter-associated modules and finite-length modules satisfying the displayed condition.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Problem9.5.3; role=supporting]\n\nAn equivalence from a module category determined by the parameter to the category determined by the simple module.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Problem9.5.3; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.5. Blocks\u0009Blocks and indecomposable central idempotents\u0009Blocks and indecomposable central idempotents\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___5___-Blocks/Blocks-and-indecomposable-central-idempotents/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___5___-Blocks--Blocks-and-indecomposable-central-idempotents--Blocks-and-indecomposable-central-idempotents--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"}});